IAggregateRoot simply marks the entry point to a group of domain objects, ensuring that all changes go through the root entity.

Think of an aggregate like a bank account.
BankAccount
 ├─ Transactions
 └─ Balance
Only the root can change state:
account.Deposit()
account.Withdraw()

Aggregates protect internal consistency.

They do NOT manage external dependencies.

External dependencies belong to:
	•	Application services
	•	Domain services
	•	Infrastructure layer

LoanCase (Aggregate Root)
 ├─ LoanApplicant
 ├─ LoanTerms
 └─ PaymentSchedule
Application Service:
var rate = rateService.GetRate(fondskode);

var loanCase = LoanCase.Create(
    applicant,
    amount,
    rate
);
loanCaseRepository.Save(loanCase);

It is usually just a marker interface:
public interface IAggregateRoot
{
}
It helps infrastructure code know:
This entity is the root of an aggregate.
Example repository rule:
public interface IRepository<T>
    where T : IAggregateRoot
{
    Task<T> Get(Guid id);
    Task Save(T entity);
}
This prevents repositories like:
LoanApplicantRepository ❌
PaymentScheduleRepository ❌
Only this is allowed
LoanCaseRepository ✅

loanCaseRepository.Save(loanCase);
